<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page5-ultimatum-water-pollution" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Finishing Zero-Sum Games, the Ultimatum Minigame, and Water Pollution</title>

  <p>
    This page covers three sections. First we'll finish Section 5.5 by
    seeing what Theorem 5.6 tells us about One-Card Two-Round Poker. Then
    come two more examples of finding mixed strategy Nash equilibria with
    the Fundamental Theorem (Theorem 5.2).
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section 5.5, continued: interchangeability and poker         -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    A quick recap from the last page. In a two-player <term>zero-sum</term>
    game, the players' payoffs add up to <m>0</m> in every cell. Theorem 5.6
    says that in these games, Nash equilibria and maximin equilibria are
    exactly the same thing, and at every Nash equilibrium, each player gets
    their maximin payoff.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before you read on, see if you can work out a consequence of Theorem 5.6
    on your own.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-interchangeability">
    <title>Interchangeability</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> and <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m> are
        both Nash equilibria of a two-player zero-sum game. Is
        <m>(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m> also a Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. By Theorem 5.6, <m>\sigma_1</m> and <m>\tau_2</m> are both
            maximin strategies, so <m>(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m> is a maximin
            equilibrium, and therefore a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! You can mix and match halves of Nash equilibria in a
            zero-sum game. By part (2), all of them give the same payoffs,
            too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            There's no way to tell without seeing the payoffs.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Theorem 5.6 answers this for every two-player zero-sum game, no
            matter what the payoffs are. Which part of the theorem connects
            Nash equilibria and maximin strategies?
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So Nash equilibria of zero-sum games have an
    <em>interchangeability</em> property: you can swap halves of two Nash
    equilibria and get another one.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-5-rest">
    <title>Section 5.5, The Rest</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Finish reading Section 5.5.</alert> Pick up right after the
      statement of Theorem 5.6. You can skip or skim the book's proof, since
      we did the main part of it on the last page.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first paragraph after the proof is the interchangeability property
      you just found in the checkpoint. The book then uses it to show that
      One-Card Two-Round Poker has only one Nash equilibrium. The argument
      can be hard to follow, so after you give it a first reading, we'll put
      it together step by step.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    The book's argument is a proof by contradiction. We'll build a direct
    version instead, using the interchangeability property and one more
    fact that came out of the calculations in Section 5.4.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-poker-uniqueness">
    <title>Only One Equilibrium in Poker</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <alert>Setup.</alert> In Section 5.4 we found the Nash equilibrium
        <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>, where
        <md>
          <mrow>\sigma_1^* \amp = \tfrac{1}{5}\,bb + \tfrac{2}{5}\,bf + \tfrac{2}{5}\,f</mrow>
          <mrow>\sigma_2^* \amp = \tfrac{8}{15}\,bb + \tfrac{2}{15}\,bf + \tfrac{1}{3}\,f</mrow>
        </md>
        In both, all three strategies are active (every pure strategy gets
        positive probability). At the end of Section 5.4, the book also
        points out the following.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          <alert>Fact from 5.4:</alert> In <em>any</em> Nash equilibrium of
          One-Card Two-Round Poker where Player 2 uses all three of his strategies, Player 1 must play
          <m>\sigma_1^*</m>.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Now let <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m> be <em>any</em> Nash equilibrium of
        the poker game. We want to show <m>\tau_1 = \sigma_1^*</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Your task.</alert> The argument has four lines, and each block
        is labeled with the line it belongs on. Lines 1, 2, and 3 each come in
        two versions, (a) and (b). Pick the right version of each line
        and drag it into the answer area in order.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks randomize="no">
      <block>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <p>
            <alert>1(a).</alert> By the interchangeability property from the
            Interchangeability checkpoint, <m>(\tau_1, \sigma_2^*)</m> is also
            a Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <p>
            <alert>1(b).</alert> By the interchangeability property from the
            Interchangeability checkpoint, <m>\tau_1 = \sigma_1^*</m>.
          </p>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block>
        <choice>
          <p>
            <alert>2(a).</alert> In that Nash equilibrium, Player 1 uses all
            three of her strategies.
          </p>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <p>
            <alert>2(b).</alert> In that Nash equilibrium, Player 2 uses all
            three of his strategies.
          </p>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block>
        <choice>
          <p>
            <alert>3(a).</alert> By Theorem 5.6, <m>\tau_1</m> and
            <m>\sigma_1^*</m> give Player 1 the same payoff, so they must be
            the same strategy.
          </p>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <p>
            <alert>3(b).</alert> By the fact from 5.4, Player 1 must play
            <m>\sigma_1^*</m> in that Nash equilibrium.
          </p>
        </choice>
      </block>
      <block>
        <p>
          <alert>4.</alert> So <m>\tau_1 = \sigma_1^*</m>.
        </p>
      </block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        The fact from 5.4 only applies to an equilibrium where Player 2 uses
        all three strategies. We don't know anything about <m>\tau_2</m>, so
        build an equilibrium that keeps <m>\tau_1</m> but gives Player 2 a
        strategy we do know.
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The correct lines are 1(a), 2(b), 3(b), 4. Keep <m>\tau_1</m> and swap
        in <m>\sigma_2^*</m>. By interchangeability,
        <m>(\tau_1, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium, and in it Player 2
        uses all three strategies. The fact from 5.4 says Player 1 must then
        play <m>\sigma_1^*</m>, so <m>\tau_1 = \sigma_1^*</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        1(b) is wrong because interchangeability only lets us swap halves of
        equilibria. It doesn't say the halves are equal, since a zero-sum game
        can have several different maximin strategies. 2(a) is wrong because
        we don't know anything about which strategies <m>\tau_1</m> uses.
        3(a) is wrong because two different strategies can earn the same
        payoff. Equal payoffs don't mean equal strategies.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The same argument with the players switched (build
    <m>(\sigma_1^*, \tau_2)</m> this time) shows <m>\tau_2 = \sigma_2^*</m>.
    So <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2) = (\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>, the equilibrium we
    already found. It's the only Nash equilibrium of the game!
  </p>


  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section 5.6: Ultimatum Minigame                              -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-6">
    <title>Section 5.6, The Ultimatum Minigame</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read all of Section 5.6.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      It's a short section. Pay attention to how the book uses the
      Fundamental Theorem: first on the pure strategy equilibrium
      <m>(f, r)</m>, and then on the mixed profiles
      <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Let's make sure the way the book applied the theorem is clear. The book
    looks at strategy profiles of the form <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m> with
    <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Theorem 5.2 gives a list of conditions to check,
    and which conditions show up depends on which strategies are active.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ultimatum-conditions">
    <statement>
      <p>
        To show that <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m> with <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m> is a
        Nash equilibrium, which conditions from Theorem 5.2 do we need to
        check? Here <m>\sigma_2 = qa + (1-q)r</m> is Bob's mixed strategy.
        Notice that the strict inequalities in <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m> mean
        both of Bob's strategies, <m>a</m> and <m>r</m>, are active!
      </p>
      <p>
        Drag every condition we need into the answer area. The order doesn't
        matter, and not every condition belongs!
      </p>
    </statement>
    <blocks>
      <block name="bob-eq" depends=""><m>\pi_2(f, a) = \pi_2(f, r)</m></block>
      <block name="alice-ineq" depends=""><m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_1(u, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = \pi_1(u, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_1(u, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_1(f, \sigma_2)</m></block>
      <block correct="no"><m>\pi_2(f, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_2(f, r)</m></block>
    </blocks>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Alice has only one active strategy, <m>f</m>, and one inactive
        strategy, <m>u</m>. Bob has two active strategies and no inactive
        ones. Which kind of condition does each active or inactive strategy
        give?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>\pi_2(f, a) = \pi_2(f, r)</m> and
        <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) \geq \pi_1(u, \sigma_2)</m>. Bob's two
        active strategies must earn him the same payoff (condition (1)), and
        Alice's inactive <m>u</m> can't beat her active <m>f</m> (condition
        (2)). Alice has only one active strategy, so there's no equality for
        her, and Bob has no inactive strategies, so there's no inequality for
        him.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So the equality <m>\pi_2(f, a) = \pi_2(f, r)</m> holds automatically
    (both are <m>2</m>), and all the work is in Alice's inequality, which
    comes out to <m>q \leq \frac{2}{3}</m>. Before moving on, let's make sure
    that answer makes sense.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-ultimatum-why-upper-bound">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The condition turned out to be <m>q \leq \frac{2}{3}</m>. Why does it
        make sense that this is an upper limit on <m>q</m>, rather than a
        lower limit?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>q</m> is how often Bob accepts an unfair offer. The more he
            accepts, the more tempting the unfair offer is for Alice, so
            <m>q</m> can't be too big or she'd switch to <m>u</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. The fair offer always pays Alice <m>2</m>. The unfair
            offer pays her <m>3</m> when Bob accepts and <m>0</m> when he
            rejects, so it's worth <m>3q</m> on average. Alice sticks with the
            fair offer as long as <m>2 \geq 3q</m>. In other words, Bob
            doesn't have to reject unfair offers for sure. Rejecting them at
            least <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> of the time is enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bob wants to accept unfair offers, so <m>q</m> has to stay in a
            range that keeps Bob happy.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Bob is actually indifferent here: against <m>f</m>, accepting and
            rejecting both give him <m>2</m>. The condition on <m>q</m> comes
            from <em>Alice's</em> inequality, not Bob's. Think about what
            happens to Alice's incentive to make an unfair offer as Bob
            becomes more willing to accept it.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    We're going to skip Section 5.7 (Colonel Blotto). Next up is Section
    5.8.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Section 5.8: Water Pollution 3                               -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-8">
    <title>Section 5.8, Water Pollution 3</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read all of Section 5.8.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the Water Pollution game from Section 3.3, now looking for
      mixed strategy equilibria. Keep the following in mind:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book writes the payoffs as functions of <m>(x, y, z)</m>, the
            probabilities that each firm purifies. Plugging in <m>1</m> or
            <m>0</m> for one of them means that firm plays a pure strategy.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book only writes out the first of the three indifference
            equations in full. We'll do the second one together below.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's Table 5.2 again for reference. In each triple, the payoffs are
    listed in the order (Firm 1, Firm 2, Firm 3).
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-water-pollution-firm3-g">
    <title>Water Pollution, Firm 3 purifies (probability <m>z</m>)</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Firm 2</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>y</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-y}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Firm 1</cell>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>0</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-x}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-4</m>)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <table xml:id="table-water-pollution-firm3-b">
    <title>Water Pollution, Firm 3 pollutes (probability <m>1-z</m>)</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Firm 2</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>y</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-y}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Firm 1</cell>
        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>g</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>-1</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-4</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-x}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>b</m></cell>
        <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>-4</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    The book writes out the first indifference equation,
    <m>\pi_1(1, y, z) = \pi_1(0, y, z)</m>, in full. Let's do the second one,
    <m>\pi_2(x, 1, z) = \pi_2(x, 0, z)</m>, which says Firm 2 gets the same
    payoff from <m>g</m> and <m>b</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-water-pollution-firm2-long-form">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Here's the left side, Firm 2's payoff if it purifies:
        <me>
          \pi_2(x, 1, z) = -1\cdot xz - 1\cdot (1-x)z - 1\cdot x(1-z) - 4\cdot(1-x)(1-z).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Now you do the right side, Firm 2's payoff if it pollutes. Remember
        that Firm 2's payoff is the <em>second</em> entry in each triple.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\pi_2(x, 0, z) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" answer="0" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot xz \;+</m>
        <fillin name="b2" answer="-3" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot (1-x)z \;+</m>
        <fillin name="b3" answer="-3" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot x(1-z) \;+</m>
        <fillin name="b4" answer="-3" width="3"/>
        <m>\cdot (1-x)(1-z)</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate name="b1">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-1"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's Firm 1's or Firm 3's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is the
              second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Now Firm 2 pollutes, so use column <m>b</m>. The <m>xz</m> term
            is the cell where Firms 1 and 3 both purify.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b2">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-4"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the third entry, Firm 3's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is
              the second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Use column <m>b</m> of the <q>Firm 3 purifies</q> table, row
            <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b3">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp value="-4"/>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That's the first entry, Firm 1's payoff. Firm 2's payoff is
              the second entry.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Use column <m>b</m> of the <q>Firm 3 pollutes</q> table, row
            <m>g</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate name="b4">
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Use column <m>b</m> of the <q>Firm 3 pollutes</q> table, row
            <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Setting the two payoffs equal gives
        <me>
          -xz - (1-x)z - x(1-z) - 4(1-x)(1-z)
          = 0xz - 3(1-x)z - 3x(1-z) - 3(1-x)(1-z).
        </me>
        Compare this with the book's version of Firm 1's equation. It has
        exactly the same shape, with <m>y</m> replaced by <m>x</m>. That's
        the symmetry of the game at work: from Firm 2's point of view, the
        situation is the same as Firm 1's. That's why this equation
        simplifies to <m>1 - 3x - 3z + 6xz = 0</m>, the first equation in
        (5.13).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So the second indifference equation is just
    <m>\pi_2(x, 1, z) = \pi_2(x, 0, z)</m>, using the two expressions above.
    The third one, <m>\pi_3(x, y, 1) = \pi_3(x, y, 0)</m>, works the same
    way. Once you're comfortable with that, the rest is solving the three
    equations, as the book does.
  </p>

  <p>
    You might wonder whether we also need an equation saying the
    probabilities add up to <m>1</m>. We don't: writing each firm's strategy
    as <m>xg + (1-x)b</m> already builds that in. There are also no
    inequalities to check, since every strategy is active. 
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-5" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.5" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
